Relevant columns and their potential use in solving the task  

• "type": filter only rows that are watch events (value = 'WatchEvent').  
• "repo": JSON/VARIANT that carries the repository name; extract the field `"name"`.  
• "repo_name": repository identifier in the files / contents sample tables; use to join against the name coming from the YEAR tables.  
• "path": full file path; restrict to paths that end in '.py'.  
• "size": file size; restrict to values < 15000.  
• "content": actual file text; search for the substring 'def '.  
• "id": blob‑SHA of the file blob; join between SAMPLE_FILES and SAMPLE_CONTENTS.  

Typical conditions that will be handy  

• WHERE "type" = 'WatchEvent'  
• WHERE "path" ILIKE '%.py'  
• WHERE "size" < 15000  
• WHERE "content" ILIKE '%def %'  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL statements (each limited to 20 rows) that progressively build toward the final answer.  
All identifiers are double‑quoted, and each statement is preceded by a short description.

```sql
-- 1. Look at raw 2017 GitHub‑Archive event rows
SELECT *
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2017"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Show only the event "type" values that occur in 2017
SELECT DISTINCT "type"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2017"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect the raw JSON stored in the "repo" column for a few events
SELECT "repo"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2017"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Extract repository names from the JSON and list a few of them
SELECT DISTINCT "repo":"name"::STRING AS "repo_name"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2017"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Retrieve 20 watch events from 2017 together with their repository name
SELECT "repo":"name"::STRING AS "repo_name"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2017"
WHERE "type" = 'WatchEvent'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Look at sample file records whose paths end with .py
SELECT "repo_name",
       "path",
       "id"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."SAMPLE_FILES"
WHERE "path" ILIKE '%.py'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Display small Python files (<15 000 bytes) together with their size
SELECT f."repo_name",
       f."path",
       c."size"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."SAMPLE_FILES"  f
JOIN "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."SAMPLE_CONTENTS" c
  ON f."id" = c."id"
WHERE f."path" ILIKE '%.py'
  AND c."size" < 15000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Add a simple content filter to make sure the file holds at least one 'def '
SELECT f."repo_name",
       f."path",
       c."size"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."SAMPLE_FILES"  f
JOIN "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."SAMPLE_CONTENTS" c
  ON f."id" = c."id"
WHERE f."path" ILIKE '%.py'
  AND c."size" < 15000
  AND c."content" ILIKE '%def %'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Count how many small Python‑with‑def files each repo has
SELECT f."repo_name",
       COUNT(*) AS "small_py_file_cnt"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."SAMPLE_FILES"  f
JOIN "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."SAMPLE_CONTENTS" c
  ON f."id" = c."id"
WHERE f."path" ILIKE '%.py'
  AND c."size" < 15000
  AND c."content" ILIKE '%def %'
GROUP BY f."repo_name"
ORDER BY "small_py_file_cnt" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Combine the 2017 watch‑event counts with the Python‑file filter
WITH watched AS (
    SELECT "repo":"name"::STRING AS "repo_name",
           COUNT(*) AS "watch_events_2017"
    FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2017"
    WHERE "type" = 'WatchEvent'
    GROUP BY "repo_name"
),
has_small_py AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT f."repo_name"
    FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."SAMPLE_FILES"  f
    JOIN "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."SAMPLE_CONTENTS" c
      ON f."id" = c."id"
    WHERE f."path" ILIKE '%.py'
      AND c."size" < 15000
      AND c."content" ILIKE '%def %'
)
SELECT w."repo_name",
       w."watch_events_2017"
FROM watched w
JOIN has_small_py p
  ON w."repo_name" = p."repo_name"
ORDER BY w."watch_events_2017" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

The last query delivers the repositories that satisfy the Python‑file criteria and orders them by the total number of 2017 watch events (ready to be cut down to the top three).
